  ta.sv  cmp-EQUAL.  § genSyntaxSizeDim (ModuleEmit ~22001).  A SIZE-form
        unpacked dim `[N]` IS `[0:N-1]`, and the syntax-driven DEF-view
        gen-block emitter skipped it entirely (it decoded only
        SimpleRangeSelect), leaving the array_typespec RANGELESS.  Surelog's
        shape: the range spans the SIZE EXPRESSION (not the brackets), the
        left bound is a location-less UINT 0 at 64 bits, and the right bound
        is an UNFOLDED vpiSubOp — at the same span — over the size expression
        AS WRITTEN (a bare ref_obj for a parameter read) and an UNPARENTED
        INT 1.  The array ts therefore ends one character short of the `]` a
        `[a:b]` dim reaches.  Measured across the literal / parameter-named /
        two-dim forms, in the TAKEN and the NOT-TAKEN branch alike.
        Corpus: ibex_alu.sv:406 `logic [5:0] bitcnt_partial [32];` inside
        `if (RV32B) begin : g_alu_rvb` — 4 heads.

  tb.sv  § genSyntaxSimplePattern (TypeEmit emitSyntaxExpr) — a POSITIONAL
        pattern ('{a,b,c,d}) is a plain operation 75 over the item
        expressions, no tagged_pattern wrapper and no member typespec; the arm
        returned null for anything but a StructuredAssignmentPattern.  The
        value constants stay UNPARENTED; a NESTED pattern's inner operation
        KEEPS its parent and its own constants unparent in turn.
        ...and § genLpUnpackedArray (ModuleEmit ~21777): an UNPACKED-ARRAY gen
        localparam is described by its ELEMENT typespec — a logic_typespec at
        the declared-type span carrying the PACKED dims — with the UNPACKED
        dims riding the PARAMETER's own vpiRange and a vpiSize of the TOTAL
        element count (`[2][2]` -> 4, the PRODUCT).  makeParamTs describes the
        whole array and fell back to a rangeless int_typespec.
        Corpus: ibex_alu.sv:712 `localparam logic [31:0] SHUFFLE_MASK_L [0:3]
        = '{32'h00ff_0000, ...};`, same gen block.  work@ibex_alu became
        cmp-EQUAL; heads 73 -> 71.

  tc.sv  BYTE-IDENTICAL.  § enumSubstDecBare (ExprEmit enumMemberSubstConst).
        A BASED-DECIMAL enum member (`XV_STD = 4'd4`) loses its base in the
        SUBSTITUTED copy: golden writes decompile "4" at INT:4 / vpiIntConst /
        size 4 while the ENUM_CONST object itself keeps `4'd4` on both sides.
        Only the decimal base drops — a BIN member (`2'b11`, ct 3) and a HEX
        one (`4'h6`, ct 5) keep their spelling in the substituted copy, so the
        rule keys on the ENCODING, not on the member.  True in every view.
